{"schema": "workere-benchmark-suite/v1", "protocol": "workere-historical-relations/v1", "config": {"seed": 42, "active_dataset": "causal_timebank", "datasets": {"causal_timebank": {"name": "CausalTimeBank-standard-eci", "repo_id": "Nofing/CausalTimeBank-standard-eci-workere", "split": "train", "text_field": "text", "ann_field": "annots", "max_examples": 0, "kfold": {"enabled": true, "n_folds": 10}, "constrain_to_pair_list": false}}, "data": {"shuffle_docs": true, "shuffle_pair_list": true, "reshuffle_per_resample": true, "binary_undirected": false, "vote_to_binary": false, "binary_default": "norel", "novote_norel": true, "labels": ["causes", "causedby", "norel"]}, "experiment": {"resampling": {"enabled": true, "n_runs": 7, "tie_breaking": "norel", "distinct_fewshots": true}}}, "documents": [{"id": "wsj_0781", "idx": 145, "text": "Intel Corp.'s most powerful computer chip <ei1 has> flaws that could <ei2 delay> several computer makers ' marketing <ei3 efforts> , but the \" bugs \" are n't <ei4 expected> to <ei5 hurt> Intel and most computer makers . Computer experts familiar with the flaws , found in Intel 's 80486 chip , <ei6 say> the defects do n't <ei7 affect> the average user and are likely to be <ei8 cleared> up before most computers using the chip as their \" brains \" <ei9 appear> on the market sometime next year . Intel <ei11 said> that last week a customer <ei13 discovered> two flaws in its 80486 microprocessor chip 's \" floating-point unit \" , a set of circuits that do certain calculations . On Friday , Intel <ei15 began> <ei16 notifying> customers about the bugs which <ei17 cause> the chip to <ei18 give> wrong answers for some mathematical calculations . But while International Business Machines Corp. and Compaq Computer Corp. <ei19 say> the bugs will <ei20 delay> products , most big computer makers <ei21 said> the flaws do n't <ei22 affect> them . \" Bugs like this are just a normal part of product development , \" <ei23 said> Richard Archuleta , director of Hewlett - Packard Co.'s advanced systems development . Hewlett <ei24 announced> last week that it <ei26 planned> to <ei27 ship> a computer based on the 486 chip early next year . \" These bugs do n't <ei29 affect> our schedule at all , \" he <ei30 said> . Likewise , AST Research Inc. and Sun Microsystems Inc. <ei31 said> the bugs wo n't <ei32 delay> their <ei33 development> of 486-based machines . \" We have n't <ei34 modified> our schedules in any way , \" <ei35 said> a Sun spokesman . To <ei36 switch> to another vendor 's chips , \" would not definitely be an option , \" he <ei37 said> . Nonetheless , <ei38 concern> about the chip may have been responsible for a <ei39 decline> of 87.5 cents in Intel 's stock to $ 32 a share yesterday in over-the-counter <ei41 trading> , on volume of 3,609,800 shares , and partly responsible for a <ei42 drop> in Compaq 's stock in New York Stock Exchange composite <ei43 trading> on Wednesday . Yesterday , Compaq <ei46 plunged> further , <ei47 closing> at $ 100 a share , off $ 8.625 a share , on volume of 2,633,700 shares . Most of Compaq 's <ei48 decline> is being <ei49 attributed> to a third-quarter earnings report that came in at the low end of analysts ' expectations . Intel <ei51 said> it had <ei52 corrected> the problems and would <ei53 start> <ei54 producing> bugless chips next week . \" We should not be <ei56 seeing> any more , \" <ei57 said> Bill Rash , Intel 's director for the 486 chip . What 's more , the bugs only <ei58 emerge> on esoteric applications such as computer-aided design and scientific calculations , he <ei59 said> , and then very seldom . \" These errata do not <ei60 affect> business programs , \" he <ei61 said> . The bugs will <ei62 cause> problems in \" specific and rare circumstances that will not occur in typical applications \" such as word-processing and spreadsheets , <ei63 said> Michael Slater , editor of the Microprocessor Report , an industry newsletter . Sun , Hewlett - Packard and others <ei64 say> Intel is n't wholly to blame for the snafu . The real culprits , they <ei65 said> , are computer makers such as IBM that have <ei66 jumped> the gun to <ei67 unveil> 486-based products . \" The reason this is <ei69 getting> so much visibility is that some <ei70 started> <ei71 shipping> and <ei72 announced> early availability , \" <ei73 said> Hewlett - Packard 's Mr. Archuleta . \" You can do that but you 're taking a risk . Those companies are paying the price for taking the risk . \" In late September , IBM <ei75 began> <ei76 shipping> a plug-in card that converts its PS / 2 model 70-A21 from a 80386 machine to an 80486 machine . An IBM spokeswoman <ei77 said> the company <ei78 told> customers Monday about the bugs and temporarily <ei80 stopped> <ei81 shipping> the product . IBM <ei82 has> no plans to <ei83 recall> its add-on cards , the spokeswoman <ei84 said> , and could probably <ei85 circumvent> the bugs without long product <ei86 delays> . \" We do n't <ei87 look> at this as a major problem for us , \" she <ei88 said> . Compaq , which <ei89 said> it <ei90 discovered> the bugs , still <ei91 plans> to <ei92 announce> new 486 products on Nov . 6 . Because of the glitch , however , the company <ei95 said> it does n't <ei96 know> when its machine will be commercially <ei97 available> . That 's a break from Compaq tradition , because the company does n't announce products until they 're actually at the dealers . The problem is being <ei99 ballyhooed> , experts <ei100 say> , because the 486 is Intel 's future flagship . Intel 's microprocessors are the chips of choice in many of today 's personal computers and the 80486 microprocessor is the spearhead of the company 's bid to guard that spot in the next generation of machines . \" Although these sorts of bugs are not at all uncommon , the 486 is an extremely high-profile product , \" <ei102 said> Mr. Slater , the newsletter editor . Intel 's 80486 chip is the Corvette of Intel 's microprocessors , a super-fast , super-expensive chip that only the most power-hungry computer users are likely to buy for at least several years . Unveiled last April , the chip crams 1.2 million transistors on a sliver of silicon , more than four times as many as on Intel 's earlier model , 80386 . Intel clocks the chip 's speed at 15 million instructions per second , or MIPs . That 's four times as fast as the 386 . Machines <ei105 using> the 486 are <ei106 expected> to <ei107 challenge> higher-priced work stations and minicomputers in applications such as so-called servers , which connect groups of computers together , and in computer-aided design . But while the chip 's speed in processing power is dazzling , it 's real strength lies in its software inheritance . The 486 is the descendant of a long series of Intel chips that <ei108 began> <ei109 dominating> the market ever since IBM <ei110 picked> the 16-bit 8088 chip for its first personal computer . ( A 16-bit microprocessor processes 16 pieces of data at a time and is slower than newer , 32-bit chips . ) Since then , Intel has <ei111 cornered> a large part of the market with successive generations of 16-bit and 32-bit chips , all of which can run software written for previous models . That 's what will <ei112 keep> computer makers <ei113 coming> in spite of the irritation of bugs . Big personal computer makers and many makers of engineering workstations are <ei114 developing> 486-based machines , which are <ei115 expected> to <ei116 reach> the market early next year . Of the big computer makers , only Apple Computer Co. bases its machines on Motorola chips instead . \" The 486 is going to <ei118 have> a big <ei119 impact> on the industry , \" <ei120 said> Hewlett - Packard 's Mr. Archuleta . \" It 's going to be the leading edge technology in personal computers for the next few years . This bug is not going to <ei122 have> any affect on that at all . \" Andy Zipser in Dallas contributed to this article .", "gold": [["ei38", "causes", "ei